From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:38:44 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] wayland: Implement wlr-output-management-unstable-v1

Add zwlr_output_manager_v1 (version 4), so output configuration tools
such as wlr-randr, kanshi, way-displays and wdisplays can list the
monitors and change their mode, position, transform, scale, adaptive
sync state and whether they are enabled.

Every monitor that is not reserved for leasing is advertised as a head
with its connector name, display name, make, model, serial, physical
size and modes. The fixed and variable refresh rate variants of a mode
are one zwlr_output_mode_v1; the variable one is selected through the
head's adaptive sync state. When the monitor configuration changes, only
the properties that changed are sent, followed by done with a new
serial; unplugged monitors and vanished modes get 'finished'.

A configuration is turned into one complete MetaMonitorsConfig covering
every monitor, checked like a configuration coming in over
org.gnome.Mutter.DisplayConfig (meta_verify_monitors_config() and
meta_monitor_manager_is_config_applicable(), now exported) and applied
in one meta_monitor_manager_apply_monitors_config() call, so changes to
several heads are atomic. 'test' uses the VERIFY method, which assigns
CRTCs without applying anything; 'apply' uses TEMPORARY, so the change is
not stored in monitors.xml, as on wlroots compositors. Properties a
client does not set keep their current values; a newly enabled head gets
its preferred mode and default scale and goes to the right of the other
heads. Heads covering the same area become one mirrored logical monitor.
The layout is moved to start at 0,0, as Mutter requires. Configurations
with gaps or overlaps, modes the monitor does not have (custom modes are
accepted when they match one of its modes), scales the backend does not
support (a scale within 1/8 of a supported one is rounded to it) or no
enabled head get 'failed'; configurations made against an outdated
serial get 'cancelled'. The monitors.xml policy that disables D-Bus
configuration disables this protocol too.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
